diff --git a/docs/backend/CPU-NUMA.md b/docs/backend/CPU-NUMA.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..8a36b27b5827fd3d6af00a9d791d7922b861e8bf
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/backend/CPU-NUMA.md
@@ -0,0 +1,95 @@
+# Linux NUMA CPU devices
+
+The CPU backend can optionally expose one selectable device per Linux NUMA
+node. This makes the existing Meta tensor-split backend usable across CPU
+sockets while keeping weights and worker threads local to each node.
+
+The feature is disabled by default. Set the environment before the process
+starts:
+
+| Variable | Default | Purpose |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| `GGML_CPU_NUMA_DEVICES` | off | Expose `CPU-NUMA0`, `CPU-NUMA1`, ... devices. |
+| `GGML_CPU_NUMA_THREADS` | min(10, physical cores/node) | Physical worker cores used by each device. |
+| `GGML_CPU_NUMA_POLL` | 50 | Thread-pool polling level, capped at 100. |
+| `GGML_CPU_NUMA_HUGEPAGES` | off | Apply `MADV_HUGEPAGE` to node-local mappings. Benchmark before enabling. |
+| `GGML_CPU_NUMA_DIRECT_ALLREDUCE` | off | Use the direct host-memory F32 collective when supported. |
+
+Device discovery respects the process CPU-affinity mask and selects one
+logical CPU per physical core. Buffers are allocated with a strict Linux
+`mbind()` policy. Device memory reporting is node-local and includes
+reclaimable file cache rather than reporting total host RAM for every node.
+
+## Server example
+
+```bash
+export GGML_CPU_NUMA_DEVICES=1
+export GGML_CPU_NUMA_THREADS=12
+export GGML_CPU_NUMA_POLL=100
+export GGML_CPU_NUMA_DIRECT_ALLREDUCE=1
+
+llama-server \
+  --model "$MODEL" \
+  --gpu-layers all \
+  --device CPU-NUMA0,CPU-NUMA1,CPU-NUMA2,CPU-NUMA3 \
+  --split-mode tensor \
+  --tensor-split 1,1,1,1 \
+  --fit off \
+  --threads 12 \
+  --threads-batch 12
+```
+
+`llama-bench` uses `/` between values because commas select parameter sweep
+values:
+
+```bash
+llama-bench \
+  --model "$MODEL" \
+  --n-prompt 0 \
+  --n-gen 32 \
+  --repetitions 3 \
+  --threads 12 \
+  --n-gpu-layers 999 \
+  --device CPU-NUMA0/CPU-NUMA1/CPU-NUMA2/CPU-NUMA3 \
+  --split-mode tensor \
+  --tensor-split 1/1/1/1
+```
+
+## Direct all-reduce contract
+
+The direct collective is deliberately narrow. It activates only when every
+backend is a `CPU-NUMA*` device and every reduction tensor is contiguous F32,
+has the same shape, and belongs to its corresponding NUMA device. It waits for
+all socket workers, reduces directly through host-addressable memory, and
+replicates the result. Any unsupported type, layout, or backend returns to the
+Meta backend's generic butterfly implementation.
+
+Meta host-backed buffers still require per-device view mapping. Treating a
+Meta buffer as an ordinary host buffer can leak its placeholder address into a
+worker graph, notably for hybrid recurrent-state views. The Meta backend also
+establishes a completion boundary before graph metadata can be recycled by the
+scheduler.
+
+## Example measurements
+
+The following decode-only results used a 27.3B, 31.45 GB GGUF on four Intel
+Xeon Gold 6242 sockets. Each value is the mean of three 32-token runs with Q8_0
+KV cache and Flash Attention enabled.
+
+| Configuration | Tokens/s |
+| --- | ---: |
+| One NUMA device, 16 cores | 2.524 |
+| Four devices, generic collective, 16 cores/socket | 5.239 |
+| Four devices, direct collective, 16 cores/socket | 6.091 |
+| Direct collective, 10 cores/socket, poll 50 | 6.783 |
+| Direct collective, 12 cores/socket, poll 50 | 7.054 |
+| Direct collective, 12 cores/socket, poll 100 | **7.169** |
+| Same peak profile with huge-page advice | 6.989 |
+
+The direct-collective output matched the generic fallback byte-for-byte for a
+fixed-seed greedy generation. In a production-style MTP smoke test, the same
+12-core profile generated 14.06-14.27 tokens/s with 90-94% draft acceptance;
+the exact-output validation completed correctly.
+
+Core count, polling, and huge pages are workload- and platform-dependent.
+Measure them independently for each model and quantization.
